  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   We propose a method based on periodic function templates with optimization to improve the current Erd\u0151s minimum overlap bound. The central idea is that optimal solutions often exhibit some form of symmetry or periodicity. We will explore multiple templates with different periods and compositions and optimize each template using constrained optimization. This approach leverages the observation that good solutions tend to be regular and repeating, and we systematically search across different structural possibilities. The key is to define candidate functions via periodic/base components and then tune them to meet the area constraint while minimizing the maximum overlap.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - Representation: Define a step function h: [0,2] -> [0,1] as a periodic function with known period/pattern or an arbitrary function within [0,1].\n   - Objective: minimize max_k \u222b[0,2] h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx (scaled appropriately)\n   - Constraint: \u222b[0,2] h(x) dx = 1\n   - Templates: We consider several periodic templates:\n        * T1(x): Piecewise constant with three values (like a balanced bipartite pattern)\n        * T2(x): Sawtooth-periodic function (approximating a sine curve)\n        * T3(x): Mirrored sawtooth (antisymmetric pattern)\n        * T4(x): Window-modulated (bin-like periods)\n   - Each template defines h(x) using parameters a,b and optionally a grid length n_grid\n   - The function is then upsampled to n sample points\n   - Optimization: For each template, we minimize the maximum overlap using projected gradient descent with constraint handling\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   The implementation will:\n   - Define all templates (wavenet)\n   - For each template, perform an optimization to find best parameters\n   - Keep the best solution across all templates\n   - Project solutions to [0,1]xsum=1\n   - Use smart optimization: \n        \u2022 Initial point from template\n        \u2022 Projected gradient descent\n        \u2022 Process of 10 template optimizations with 300 GD iterations each\n        \u2022 If initial parent exists, use it as fallback\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   - We'll consider 4 templates with increasing complexity\n   - For each template, we optimize parameters using:\n        \u2022 Gradient descent with line search\n        \u2022 Step size adaptation\n        \u2022 Multiple restarts\n        \u2022 Full analytical gradient\n   - Key parameters:\n        \u2022 Template parameters ordering: amplitude and offset\n        \u2022 Periodic grid resolution\n        \u2022 Number of periods in [0,2]\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - Each template optimization: 300 iterations with 2 correlation computations\n   - Total evaluations: 4 templates \u00d7 300 = 1200 function evaluations\n   - Grad descent parameters: \n        \u2022 Initial learning rate = 0.01\n        \u2022 Minimum element = 0.0001\n        \u2022 Max iteration = 300\n   - Early termination if stuck\n   - Final validation: check boundedness and area constraint before return\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Always validate:\n        o h_values in [0,1] after projection\n        o Area exactly 1 (to within floating point tolerance)\n        o Objective has finite positive value\n        o Feasibility: attempt re-projection if needed\n   - Compute final c5 = max correlation * dx\n   - Repair feasibility with projection before final evaluation\n   - Use best_c_best across templates as the metric\n   - For unknown parent, proceed with blank template search",
